270 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534877 17592186045417 true] [17592186442270 1077 "selection" 13194139534877 17592186045417 true] [17592186442270 1078 #edb/ref 17592186442266 13194139534877 17592186045417 true] [17592186442270 1079 #uuid "d8e6f41c-9e5c-55de-9517-8eaa2b14ab20" 13194139534877 17592186045417 true] [17592186442271 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442269 13194139534877 17592186045417 true] [17592186442271 1069 "q-37356" 13194139534877 17592186045417 true] [17592186442271 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534877 17592186045417 true] [17592186442271 1071 "$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $B$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\lambda =-5$ is an eigenvalue of $B$\n\nII. $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvalue of $B$\n\nIII. The eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ has algebraic multiplicity $2$" 13194139534877 17592186045417 true] [17592186442271 1072 #uuid "970b7afa-4ca1-5d4a-a4eb-b7279227d558" 13194139534877 17592186045417 true] [17592186442271 1074 "First, we find the matrix $\\,B-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nB-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}-5-\\lambda  & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3-\\lambda  & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(B-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nNotice that we can expand the $4\\times 4$ determinant across the $1$st column, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-5-\\lambda  & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3-\\lambda  & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-5-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & 0 & -2 \\\\ 7 & -3-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, expanding the $3\\times 3$ determinant across the $2$nd column, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-5-\\lambda )\\cdot (-3-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 5 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-5-\\lambda )\\cdot (-3-\\lambda )((3-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda )+10) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +5)(\\lambda +3)({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +7) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D<0$ for the polynomial ${\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +7$, we have that the only real eigenvalues are $\\lambda =-5$ and $\\lambda =-3$ (both with algebraic multiplicity $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534877 17592186045417 true] [17592186442272 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442270 13194139534877 17592186045417 true] [17592186442272 1069 "q-140475" 13194139534877 17592186045417 true] [17592186442272 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534877 17592186045417 true] [17592186442272 1071 "Determine all real eigenvalues $\\lambda $ of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139534877 17592186045417 true] [17592186442272 1072 #uuid "bedee111-5fec-56ad-a271-6904f3e057b2" 13194139534877 17592186045417 true] [17592186442272 1074 "First, we find the matrix $\\,A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is given by $det(A-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nExpanding the determinant across the $1$st row, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-3-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}|-(-1)|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}|+(-1)|\\begin{aligned}1 & -3-\\lambda  \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-{\\lambda }^{3}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+15\\lambda )+(-5-\\lambda )+(5+\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ -{\\lambda }^{3}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+13\\lambda -10 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +10 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to solve this equation. We find the first root by testing some values. According to the rational zeros theorem, a list of good first guesses consists of all divisors of $10:$ \n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\\pm 5,\\pm 10\n$$\n\nLet's pick $x=1.$ Substituting to the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1)^{3}+2(1)^{2}-13(1)+10 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lambda =1$ is a root of the equation. So, we can factor $(\\lambda -1)$ out of ${\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +10$ using synthetic division:\n\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-13$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $↓$ | $1$ | $3$ | $-10$ |\n|  | $1$ | $3$ | $-10$ | $0$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda -1)({\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -10) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +5)(\\lambda -2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-5,$ $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=1,\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=2.$" 13194139534877 17592186045417 true] [17592186442273 1062 #uuid "3399fe8e-8176-5e35-93d5-9a34329ea9c3" 13194139534877 17592186045417 true] [17592186442273 1063 #edb/ref 17592186272116 13194139534877 17592186045417 true] [17592186442274 1058 #edb/ref 17592186442277 13194139534877 17592186045417 true] [17592186442274 1062 #uuid "34fc937b-c4ca-51d0-b41f-cc48a4e031b8" 13194139534877 17592186045417 true] [17592186442274 1063 #edb/ref 17592186442271 13194139534877 17592186045417 true] [17592186442275 1058 #edb/ref 17592186442273 13194139534877 17592186045417 true] [17592186442275 1062 #uuid "41fe8de5-4b38-5378-88a7-29da8122c672" 13194139534877 17592186045417 true] [17592186442275 1063 #edb/ref 17592186442272 13194139534877 17592186045417 true] [17592186442276 1058 #edb/ref 17592186442274 13194139534877 17592186045417 true] [17592186442276 1062 #uuid "4407ab4d-760d-5929-a4aa-ceca8bb19594" 13194139534877 17592186045417 true] [17592186442276 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125062 13194139534877 17592186045417 true] [17592186442277 1058 #edb/ref 17592186442275 131